running Kubernetes,

00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:15.950
you may recognize them.

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:17.900
So you go to set up
Kubernetes and you build

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:20.585
yourself a cluster of machines.

00:13:20.585 --> 00:13:22.460
You want to make sure
you have attached disks

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:24.650
to the node machines,
because they're

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:27.830
going to need extra storage
to run your containers

00:13:27.830 --> 00:13:29.247
and whatnot.

00:13:29.247 --> 00:13:31.580
And then you're going to go
ahead and install Kubernetes

00:13:31.580 --> 00:13:33.950
and wire up all the
connections to make

00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:36.590
sure you have a network that
will allow all of them to talk.

00:13:36.590 --> 00:13:38.120
You want to make sure
you have your nodes.

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:39.370
You want to make sure
you have your masters.

00:13:39.370 --> 00:13:41.745
You want to make sure they're
all talking to one another.

00:13:41.745 --> 00:13:45.350
And then you're going to want to
add DNS and service discovery.

00:13:45.350 --> 00:13:47.780
And then probably someone
will say, how do we

00:13:47.780 --> 00:13:48.750
know stuff is running?

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:52.093
So you got to add
logging and monitoring.

00:13:52.093 --> 00:13:54.510
And then you got to make sure
the OSs all stay up to date.

00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:56.940
Then you got to make sure
Kubernetes stays up to date.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:14:00.490
And that's just a
lot of work, right?

00:14:00.490 --> 00:14:01.490
Like it's a lot of work.

