00:21:13.573 --> 00:21:14.990
And now we have a
question to ask.

00:21:14.990 --> 00:21:16.590
It's like, which
do I choose, right?

00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:18.820
Like I've got
containers, I've got VMs,

00:21:18.820 --> 00:21:19.820
and I've got serverless.

00:21:19.820 --> 00:21:22.640
Which one do I choose?

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:25.820
And that is, like I said,
an entire talk on its own.

00:21:25.820 --> 00:21:29.180
But let me try to give you kind
of the bullet points of this.

00:21:29.180 --> 00:21:31.260
If you've got a lot of
stuff in VMs and VMs

00:21:31.260 --> 00:21:33.862
are the gravity that are
holding you to being on prem

00:21:33.862 --> 00:21:35.570
and you want to move
to cloud, well then,

00:21:35.570 --> 00:21:37.460
I would just go
with Compute Engine,

00:21:37.460 --> 00:21:39.750
just do migrations
to VMs and fine.

00:21:39.750 --> 00:21:43.270
Especially if you have a large
number of systems people,

00:21:43.270 --> 00:21:46.010
if you're in a shop that's
more systems than dev,

00:21:46.010 --> 00:21:49.790
it totally makes sense to go
to VMs and not switch over.

00:21:49.790 --> 00:21:51.890
If you have a lot of
developers and you

00:21:51.890 --> 00:21:54.090
have a lot of
Greenfield projects,

00:21:54.090 --> 00:21:56.060
it's a lot easier to
start Greenfield projects

00:21:56.060 --> 00:22:00.060
on serverless than it is to
start it on your own hardware.

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:03.090
So that would kind of lend
you towards going serverless.

00:22:03.090 --> 00:22:06.948
If you have a good mix of both
systems people and developers,

00:22:06.948 --> 00:22:09.240
and you're looking to get
the most out of the hardware,

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:12.320
to get the most out of your
spend for your resources,

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:16.540
container and Container
Engine is the way I would go.

00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:19.040
And one of the advantages
of containerization

00:22:19.040 --> 00:22:24.110
is, one, preparing your apps to
run on containers pretty much

00:22:24.110 --> 00:22:26.360
matches what you need to do
to run them on serverless,

00:22:26.360 --> 00:22:29.250
except the whole Docker part.

00:22:29.250 --> 00:22:31.250
But especially with the
advent of Cloud Run,

00:22:31.250 --> 00:22:33.280
running in containers, you can
choose to run on Kubernetes.

00:22:33.280 --> 00:22:34.863
You can choose it
to run on Cloud Run.

00:22:34.863 --> 00:22:36.530
You can choose to
run it on Docker.

00:22:36.530 --> 00:22:39.113
You can choose to run on a whole
bunch of different platforms.

00:22:39.113 --> 00:22:40.880
So when in doubt, I
would say containers

00:22:40.880 --> 00:22:43.290
is probably the way
you want to go for now.

00:22:43.290 --> 00:22:44.780
Serverless, I
think, is eventually

00:22:44.780 --> 00:22:47.480
going to be what
everyone wants to do.

00:22:47.480 --> 00:22:51.170
I think it is today what a
lot of developers want to do,

00:22:51.170 --> 00:22:53.870
but there's things
blocking people from going.

00:22:53.870 --> 00:22:58.220
But I think, so a lot of VMs,
stick with Compute Engine.

00:22:58.220 --> 00:23:00.470
If you have a lot of
developers, go with serverless.

00:23:00.470 --> 00:23:02.762
If you a lot of systems people
and a lot of developers,

00:23:02.762 --> 00:23:05.480
you can go with containers
with the added thing

00:23:05.480 --> 00:23:07.190
if you can learn
containers now, it

00:23:07.190 --> 00:23:11.480
would make future transitions
for you much easier.

00:23:11.480 --> 00:23:14.113
That's a lot condensed
into one slide.

00:23:14.113 --> 00:23:16.280
All right, so I'm going to
switch gears here and now

00:23:16.280 --> 00:23:17.240
talk about networking.

00:23:17.240 --> 00:23:19.880
One of the things that we are
proudest about about Google

00:23:19.880 --> 00:23:24.440
Cloud and our infrastructure, is
we have our own private network

00:23:24.440 --> 00:23:26.030
so that when you
are transmitting

00:23:26.030 --> 00:23:29.900
data between our services,
it transmits completely

00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:33.390
on our network before it ever
touches the public internet.

00:23:33.390 --> 00:23:35.250
And we can route stuff
all around the world.

00:23:35.250 --> 00:23:38.060
So if you are a
user in Australia

00:23:38.060 --> 00:23:42.013
and you request a resource
in North America, what's

00:23:42.013 --> 00:23:43.430
going to happen
is, the request is

00:23:43.430 --> 00:23:46.670
going to go to a local point of
presence in Australia for us,

00:23:46.670 --> 00:23:49.460
and then travel along our
network to North America

00:23:49.460 --> 00:23:51.440
and then back.

00:23:51.440 --> 00:23:53.780
And what this does
is, it provides--

00:23:53.780 --> 00:23:56.840
you get shielded from a
lot of the bursting nature

00:23:56.840 --> 00:23:59.150
of internet traffic
because our network is

00:23:59.150 --> 00:24:00.410
a little bit more consistent.

00:24:00.410 --> 00:24:02.420
And you get pretty
good throughput

00:24:02.420 --> 00:24:04.490
through that back end network.

00:24:04.490 --> 00:24:08.840
And I actually want to show
a little demo of an app

00:24:08.840 --> 00:24:10.580
that we built to kind
of show this off.

00:24:10.580 --> 00:24:12.380
So if you could switch
back to the laptop.

00:24:12.380 --> 00:24:17.060
OK, so what I'm going to
do is, I have a picture--

00:24:17.060 --> 00:24:19.745
I have a head shot on my
desktop that I took earlier.

00:24:19.745 --> 00:24:22.150
So there I am.

00:24:22.150 --> 00:24:23.173
There's me.

00:24:23.173 --> 00:24:24.840
We're going to send
me around the world.

00:24:24.840 --> 00:24:25.715
So I'm going to send.

00:24:25.715 --> 00:24:27.440
We're going to prepare,
we're connecting,

00:24:27.440 --> 00:24:30.860
and then we're going to be able
to see this travel in real time

00:24:30.860 --> 00:24:33.101
across our network.

00:24:33.101 --> 00:24:34.200
Bam, we're done.

00:24:34.200 --> 00:24:38.130
So that transmit, that going
from Taiwan to Tokyo to Sydney

00:24:38.130 --> 00:24:41.700
to Oregon to Iowa to Virginia
to London to Frankfurt

00:24:41.700 --> 00:24:46.420
to Sao Paulo, took 4.24
seconds on our network.

00:24:46.420 --> 00:24:50.040
And what's great about the setup
that we have for this is, one,

00:24:50.040 --> 00:24:52.517
that's fairly good
that we're able to send

00:24:52.517 --> 00:24:53.350
this message across.

00:24:53.350 --> 00:24:54.920
And we stamped it
in all the places.

00:24:54.920 --> 00:24:56.610
We had a VM in
each location that

00:24:56.610 --> 00:24:58.440
took the image in,
stamped it, and then

00:24:58.440 --> 00:25:00.072
sent it onto the next spot.

00:25:00.072 --> 00:25:01.530
But what's really
great about this,

00:25:01.530 --> 00:25:03.467
is that I didn't have
to set up any networking

00:25:03.467 --> 00:25:04.300
to make this happen.

00:25:04.300 --> 00:25:08.550
If you have your VMs
in one of our projects,

00:25:08.550 --> 00:25:10.630
they can automatically
talk to one another.

00:25:10.630 --> 00:25:13.047
So you don't have to go to
different consoles or different

00:25:13.047 --> 00:25:13.980
parts of the world.

00:25:13.980 --> 00:25:16.015
You can just have all
of your VMs in one place

00:25:16.015 --> 00:25:17.640
and wire them up to
talk to one another

00:25:17.640 --> 00:25:20.240
just by adding them
to the same project.

00:25:20.240 --> 00:25:21.990
If you want to play
with this, we actually

00:25:21.990 --> 00:25:26.150
have a full version of this
out in the dev lounge--

00:25:26.150 --> 00:25:27.720
sorry, the dev zone.

00:25:27.720 --> 00:25:29.692
And you can actually get
selfies that print out

00:25:29.692 --> 00:25:31.650
and your picture has
traveled around the world,

00:25:31.650 --> 00:25:35.730
and you can take that
home as a souvenir.

00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:41.200
So I'm going to switch
back to the preso.

00:25:41.200 --> 00:25:42.170
I'm going backwards.

00:25:42.170 --> 00:25:42.890
There we go.

00:25:42.890 --> 00:25:44.960
All right, now some
basic things that people

00:25:44.960 --> 00:25:46.490
want from the network here.

00:25:46.490 --> 00:25:47.630
They want load balancers.

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:49.460
We have load balancers.

00:25:49.460 --> 00:25:52.340
We divide them into
HTTP SSL, which

00:25:52.340 --> 00:25:54.650
you would think of as more
than L7 load balancer.

00:25:54.650 --> 00:25:56.790
We also have network
load balancers,

00:25:56.790 --> 00:25:59.180
which is what you think
of as an L4 load balancer.

00:25:59.180 --> 00:26:00.860
You can also set up
the load balancers

00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:04.645
to be internal only so that
you can only have traffic--

00:26:04.645 --> 00:26:06.770
you don't have to expose
your services to the world

00:26:06.770 --> 00:26:09.180
to get them to be load balanced.

00:26:09.180 --> 00:26:09.950
We have firewalls.

00:26:09.950 --> 00:26:11.130
Pretty easy to set up.

00:26:11.130 --> 00:26:13.640
You set up a rule,
open port 3306.

00:26:13.640 --> 00:26:16.880
You give that rule
a name, MySQL.

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:19.790
You apply that tag,
that name to a machine,

00:26:19.790 --> 00:26:22.760
and it'll open up that
port on that machine.

00:26:22.760 --> 00:26:27.132
Relatively easy to do and
pretty simple to go through.

00:26:27.132 --> 00:26:28.590
We also have Cloud
virtual network.

00:26:28.590 --> 00:26:32.510
So if you want to configure
routes to handle communication

00:26:32.510 --> 00:26:35.630
between your Google Cloud
instances and another location,

00:26:35.630 --> 00:26:36.845
you can do that.

00:26:36.845 --> 00:26:37.970
We have Cloud Interconnect.

00:26:37.970 --> 00:26:39.170
If you have enough
traffic that you

00:26:39.170 --> 00:26:40.670
would like to
directly peer with us,

00:26:40.670 --> 00:26:43.070
that's something that
we can talk about.

00:26:43.070 --> 00:26:46.840
It is obviously something where
there's a lot of upfront cost.

00:26:46.840 --> 00:26:49.340
But the advantage is, is once
you start that pipeline going,

00:26:49.340 --> 00:26:50.923
you can transmit
large amounts of data

00:26:50.923 --> 00:26:53.960
for a better experience.

00:26:53.960 --> 00:26:54.650
VPN.

00:26:54.650 --> 00:26:57.133
We do provide VPN, but it's
gateway to gateway only.

00:26:57.133 --> 00:26:58.800
This isn't for hooking
up road warriors.

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:00.680
This is basically
used to connect you

00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:02.750
to another setup
someplace securely

00:27:02.750 --> 00:27:05.663
with your own tunnel in place.

00:27:05.663 --> 00:27:06.580
CDN, we do have a CDN.

00:27:09.290 --> 00:27:11.905
I wouldn't, this is not like
say competitive with Akamai.

00:27:11.905 --> 00:27:13.280
This is like you
just want to add

00:27:13.280 --> 00:27:15.853
some edge caching to your work.

00:27:15.853 --> 00:27:16.520
You can do that.

00:27:16.520 --> 00:27:17.870
You set it up through
our load balancers.

00:27:17.870 --> 00:27:19.850
They'll use HTTP headers
to determine whether or not

00:27:19.850 --> 00:27:20.475
stuff's cached.

00:27:20.475 --> 00:27:21.890
You can invalidate stuff.

00:27:21.890 --> 00:27:23.390
But if you need
more advanced tools,

00:27:23.390 --> 00:27:25.390
you probably want to go
with one of our partners

00:27:25.390 --> 00:27:28.480
instead of just the cloud CDN.

00:27:28.480 --> 00:27:29.420
Also DNS.

00:27:29.420 --> 00:27:32.840
I love this, because it
irritates the lawyers whenever

00:27:32.840 --> 00:27:36.260
I give this talk, because it
is the only product we have

00:27:36.260 --> 00:27:38.087
that has 100% SLA.

00:27:38.087 --> 00:27:39.920
And every single time
I give it, the lawyers

00:27:39.920 --> 00:27:41.100
say, you can't say that.

00:27:41.100 --> 00:27:44.600
And I was like, it says it
right there on the web page.

00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:48.260
I'm saying it's the only product
we have that has 100% SLA.

00:27:48.260 --> 00:27:51.050
So you can run your
DNS off of our network,

00:27:51.050 --> 00:27:53.480
and our name
servers do have 100%

00:27:53.480 --> 00:27:57.680
SLA, which means that
if you go with this

00:27:57.680 --> 00:28:01.880
and we have an outage, don't
mention me in your lawsuit,

00:28:01.880 --> 00:28:05.540
I think is what was coming
from what I just said.

00:28:05.540 --> 00:28:06.470
Great.

00:28:06.470 --> 00:28:09.120
All right, we're going
to move gears to storage.

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:13.100
So storage we basically divide
into structured or unstructured

00:28:13.100 --> 00:28:14.330
storage.

00:28:14.330 --> 00:28:18.710
Structured are things like data,
application data, user data.

00:28:18.710 --> 00:28:22.250
And unstructured, things like
files and blobs and pictures

00:28:22.250 --> 00:28:23.910
and other things.

00:28:23.910 --> 00:28:25.802
So we'll start
with unstructured.

00:28:25.802 --> 00:28:27.260
And we'll start
with Cloud Storage.

00:28:27.260 --> 00:28:29.093
Cloud Storage is object
storing and serving.

00:28:29.093 --> 00:28:30.980
If you want to have
a giant bucket where

00:28:30.980 --> 00:28:33.230
you put all your files and
serve them up to the world,

00:28:33.230 --> 00:28:35.750
this is the way you would do it.

00:28:35.750 --> 00:28:38.360
Divide this kind of
into three areas--

00:28:38.360 --> 00:28:41.850
public content, process
data and long-term storage.

00:28:41.850 --> 00:28:42.860
So we look at the chart.

00:28:42.860 --> 00:28:46.310
Public data, we have
at the bottom there,

00:28:46.310 --> 00:28:48.470
multi-regional storage.

00:28:48.470 --> 00:28:50.450
It is 99.95% available.

00:28:50.450 --> 00:28:51.500
It's geo-redundant.

00:28:51.500 --> 00:28:52.740
So this is for web content.

00:28:52.740 --> 00:28:54.740
This is for stuff you
want to push to your users

00:28:54.740 --> 00:28:57.140
as close as possible
so that they

00:28:57.140 --> 00:28:59.870
get the fastest
pull-down of the data

00:28:59.870 --> 00:29:01.370
that you possibly can give.

00:29:01.370 --> 00:29:04.910
It is 2.6 gigabytes percent--

00:29:04.910 --> 00:29:09.890
sorry, 2.6 cents per gigabyte
per month to store it there.

00:29:09.890 --> 00:29:13.100
Now that's for
public facing data.

00:29:13.100 --> 00:29:14.930
If you have large
data jobs, you've

00:29:14.930 --> 00:29:18.110
got LOB files or
batch data that you

00:29:18.110 --> 00:29:20.527
want to run a lot
of computation on,

00:29:20.527 --> 00:29:22.610
you don't necessarily need
it close to your users.

00:29:22.610 --> 00:29:24.193
You need it close
to the machines that

00:29:24.193 --> 00:29:25.440
are going to do that work.

00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:27.440
So you can go with regional,
where the data will

00:29:27.440 --> 00:29:29.870
stay in one region, but
it's OK, because you're

00:29:29.870 --> 00:29:34.140
going to set your VMs up there
to churn through them there.

00:29:34.140 --> 00:29:36.740
And so they're only
going to be-- or let's

00:29:36.740 --> 00:29:39.350
say you're just a regional,
you have a regional presence

00:29:39.350 --> 00:29:41.870
and you don't want to
necessarily serve the world.

00:29:41.870 --> 00:29:43.950
You can just set
up your data here.

00:29:43.950 --> 00:29:46.460
Now, it's important to note
that just because it's regional,

00:29:46.460 --> 00:29:48.460
doesn't mean it's only
available in that region.

00:29:48.460 --> 00:29:50.360
It just means that it
will be faster served

00:29:50.360 --> 00:29:51.818
in that region than
somewhere else.

00:29:51.818 --> 00:29:54.590
It's still worldwide available
if you wish it to be.

00:29:54.590 --> 00:29:58.640
It's cheaper. $0.02
per gigabyte per month.

00:29:58.640 --> 00:30:00.590
And so that's regional.

00:30:00.590 --> 00:30:03.890
Now we have two long term
storage implementations.

00:30:03.890 --> 00:30:08.210
One is what we call Nearline,
which is 99% available.

00:30:08.210 --> 00:30:10.190
It's got a 30-day
minimum, which means

00:30:10.190 --> 00:30:12.465
you can put data in and
delete it in under 30 days,

00:30:12.465 --> 00:30:14.090
but we're going to
charge you as if you

00:30:14.090 --> 00:30:15.180
left it there the entire time.

00:30:15.180 --> 00:30:16.555
But you can
absolutely delete it.

00:30:16.555 --> 00:30:17.870
That's totally fine.

00:30:17.870 --> 00:30:19.622
It's $0.01 per
gigabyte per month.

00:30:19.622 --> 00:30:21.080
And unlike the
other ones, there is

00:30:21.080 --> 00:30:23.270
a charge for pulling data out.

00:30:23.270 --> 00:30:27.735
So if you envision needing
this data multiple times, what

00:30:27.735 --> 00:30:29.360
you probably want to
do is pull it out,

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:33.030
leave it someplace, instead
of pulling it out repeatedly.

00:30:33.030 --> 00:30:36.320
So it's for things like backup
or maybe long tail multimedia

00:30:36.320 --> 00:30:38.210
that's not getting
a lot of use now,

00:30:38.210 --> 00:30:41.060
just leave it there and
have it be stored cheaper.

00:30:41.060 --> 00:30:43.880
The final one is Coldline,
which is our cheapest.

00:30:43.880 --> 00:30:47.810
It's 99% available,
just like Nearline.

00:30:47.810 --> 00:30:49.430
It's got a 90-day
minimum storage.

00:30:49.430 --> 00:30:50.960
Again, you can pull
it out earlier,

00:30:50.960 --> 00:30:52.370
but we'll just charge you.

00:30:52.370 --> 00:30:55.490
And this is for things like
disaster recovery or compliance

00:30:55.490 --> 00:30:58.970
archives, things that
you really hope you never

00:30:58.970 --> 00:31:02.570
have to pull back out,
because if you do,

00:31:02.570 --> 00:31:06.140
you probably have a resume
open in another window.

00:31:06.140 --> 00:31:09.170
Disaster recovery and compliance
archive-- two things that

00:31:09.170 --> 00:31:11.310
are not happy making.

00:31:11.310 --> 00:31:14.600
But if you have to, at least
it's the cheapest one to store,

00:31:14.600 --> 00:31:17.150
and you will not
kind of complain

00:31:17.150 --> 00:31:21.260
about the $0.05 per gigabyte
to pull it out, because you've

00:31:21.260 --> 00:31:23.340
got bigger problems.

00:31:23.340 --> 00:31:25.190
But the incentive here
is to try to push you

00:31:25.190 --> 00:31:28.290
towards making sure
that you're using it

00:31:28.290 --> 00:31:29.720
the way we kind of envisioned.

00:31:29.720 --> 00:31:33.530
This is not pulling stuff
in and out very quickly.

00:31:33.530 --> 00:31:37.598
This is for disaster
recovery and compliance.

00:31:37.598 --> 00:31:39.140
All right, we also
have another thing

00:31:39.140 --> 00:31:40.973
called Cloud Filestore,
which I'm not really

00:31:40.973 --> 00:31:43.015
going to go too much into,
because it's basically

00:31:43.015 --> 00:31:44.295
just a managed NFS server.

00:31:44.295 --> 00:31:46.100
If you need a file
system, if you

00:31:46.100 --> 00:31:49.878
need multiple machines to
look at the same file system,

00:31:49.878 --> 00:31:50.420
there you go.

00:31:50.420 --> 00:31:52.370
We have this product.

00:31:52.370 --> 00:31:56.362
If you need an NFS server,
this is what you need, great.

00:31:56.362 --> 00:31:58.070
So now I'm going to
talk about SQL NoSQL.

00:31:58.070 --> 00:32:00.445
Or actually I'm not going to
talk about SQL versus NoSQL,

00:32:00.445 --> 00:32:03.080
because I don't want any
part of that argument.

00:32:03.080 --> 00:32:04.130
I have my preferences.

00:32:04.130 --> 00:32:05.213
You have your preferences.

00:32:05.213 --> 00:32:08.180
Let's all just agree
to be friends and not

00:32:08.180 --> 00:32:14.520
get into a NoSQL is
web scale discussion.

00:32:14.520 --> 00:32:15.860
So we'll start with NoSQL.

00:32:15.860 --> 00:32:17.660
We have two products here.

00:32:17.660 --> 00:32:20.390
Cloud Datastore, which is a
horizontally scalable document

00:32:20.390 --> 00:32:22.580
DB, and we have Cloud
Firestore, which

00:32:22.580 --> 00:32:25.290
is a strongly-consistent
serverless document DB.

00:32:25.290 --> 00:32:28.010
And you might, one, say I
don't think the hyphen makes

00:32:28.010 --> 00:32:30.560
that four words, and I agree.

00:32:30.560 --> 00:32:32.760
The other thing you might
say is, these two things,

00:32:32.760 --> 00:32:37.700
if I look at them,
they're really similar.

00:32:37.700 --> 00:32:39.110
They're both document DBs.

00:32:39.110 --> 00:32:42.560
They're both
horizontally scalable

00:32:42.560 --> 00:32:44.170
versus strongly-consistent.

00:32:44.170 --> 00:32:45.670
All right, so what
is the difference

00:32:45.670 --> 00:32:47.300
between these two things?

00:32:47.300 --> 00:32:52.160
Basically, Datastore is a little
bit more capacity, a little bit

00:32:52.160 --> 00:32:54.740
more scale, a little
bit more robust.

00:32:54.740 --> 00:32:58.250
Firestore is based
off of Datastore,

00:32:58.250 --> 00:33:02.600
but with the Fire base kind of
APIs on the front end, which

00:33:02.600 --> 00:33:06.410
means, one, there's really great
connectivity to local clients.

00:33:06.410 --> 00:33:09.200
So the SDK for writing
to mobile and web

00:33:09.200 --> 00:33:13.010
is, I think, really, really
fantastic and easy to use.

00:33:13.010 --> 00:33:15.320
And it has real
time capabilities.

00:33:15.320 --> 00:33:17.775
In fact, that
network demo that I

00:33:17.775 --> 00:33:19.400
showed where you're
seeing in real time

00:33:19.400 --> 00:33:21.200
and hopping from
place to place, that

00:33:21.200 --> 00:33:23.720
was powered by Firestore,
because I needed that

00:33:23.720 --> 00:33:27.980
for the real time kind of
aspect of the experience.

00:33:27.980 --> 00:33:30.140
OK, so then we have Cloud SQL--

00:33:30.140 --> 00:33:33.080
Manage MySQL and PostgreSQL.

00:33:33.080 --> 00:33:33.890
That's what it is.

00:33:33.890 --> 00:33:35.720
It's just manage these things.

00:33:35.720 --> 00:33:38.210
You don't have to
worry about maintenance

00:33:38.210 --> 00:33:42.227
or you could still do all the
DBA tasks you might need to do,

00:33:42.227 --> 00:33:44.060
but you don't really
have to do systems work

00:33:44.060 --> 00:33:46.110
to keep that machine running.

00:33:46.110 --> 00:33:48.430
So that's Cloud SQL.

00:33:48.430 --> 00:33:54.080
And we have finally Cloud
Spanner, which is horizontally

00:33:54.080 --> 00:33:56.870
scalable relational
database, which it basically

00:33:56.870 --> 00:33:59.930
combines some of the best
features of NoSQL, which

00:33:59.930 --> 00:34:03.140
is the fact that you
can chart it, separate

00:34:03.140 --> 00:34:04.940
a whole bunch of
different instances,

00:34:04.940 --> 00:34:09.110
and have that drive better
performance and better scale.

00:34:09.110 --> 00:34:12.379
But it comes at a cost,
which is, it is not like a--

00:34:12.379 --> 00:34:14.975
it's more of a BMW
than a Volkswagen,

00:34:14.975 --> 00:34:17.221
if you know what I'm saying.

00:34:17.221 --> 00:34:18.679
So if you go to
experiment with it,

00:34:18.679 --> 00:34:21.380
please watch the pricing meter.

00:34:21.380 --> 00:34:24.710
Because it's a great tool when
you have that level of scale

00:34:24.710 --> 00:34:27.530
and that level of client that
you need to access or you

00:34:27.530 --> 00:34:35.000
need to give access to, but
it is a pretty robust tool.

00:34:35.000 --> 00:34:36.770
So how do I make the
decision of which

00:34:36.770 --> 00:34:38.300
one to use among all of these?

00:34:38.300 --> 00:34:40.610
Well first, it's
easy to split up.

00:34:40.610 --> 00:34:43.790
If I've got unstructured stuff,
I want to use one of the two

00:34:43.790 --> 00:34:44.310
on the left.

00:34:44.310 --> 00:34:46.980
If I have structured stuff, I
want to use it on the right.

00:34:46.980 --> 00:34:51.750
If I have pictures or
documents or other things,

00:34:51.750 --> 00:34:54.190
how do I choose between Cloud
Storage and Cloud Filestore?

00:34:54.190 --> 00:34:55.100
This is really easy.

00:34:55.100 --> 00:34:57.230
Do you need a file system
and then attach it to a VM?

00:34:57.230 --> 00:34:58.605
Then you probably
want Firestore.

00:34:58.605 --> 00:34:59.870
If not, use Cloud Storage.

00:34:59.870 --> 00:35:01.160
Great.

00:35:01.160 --> 00:35:04.280
Also, if you want to share
it with the internet,

00:35:04.280 --> 00:35:05.990
Cloud Storage is
one click to do,

00:35:05.990 --> 00:35:08.850
whereas with Cloud Filestore,
you're dependent on the VMs

00:35:08.850 --> 00:35:12.238
that you've run to run web
server software in order

00:35:12.238 --> 00:35:13.780
to serve up anything
that's on there.

00:35:13.780 --> 00:35:15.710
So I would, if you
want to share it,

00:35:15.710 --> 00:35:17.690
Cloud Storage is
probably the way to go.

00:35:17.690 --> 00:35:19.060
All right, so structured data.

00:35:19.060 --> 00:35:22.070
Let's say user data or
financial transaction data

00:35:22.070 --> 00:35:24.620
or a stack of paper,
which is, I don't know

00:35:24.620 --> 00:35:27.360
what I was going for with that.

00:35:27.360 --> 00:35:29.960
But you've got structured data.

00:35:29.960 --> 00:35:33.590
If you're going with NoSQL,
if you are talking directly

00:35:33.590 --> 00:35:36.320
between your database
and web clients,

00:35:36.320 --> 00:35:38.540
I would highly recommend
Cloud Firestore.

00:35:38.540 --> 00:35:40.790
If you're going a lot of
server, server communication,

00:35:40.790 --> 00:35:42.980
Cloud Datastore is probably
the way you want to go.

00:35:42.980 --> 00:35:45.470
And then between Cloud
SQL and Cloud Spanner,

00:35:45.470 --> 00:35:48.680
do you have a crap ton of
users all around the world

00:35:48.680 --> 00:35:51.795
and high needs for availability?

00:35:51.795 --> 00:35:54.170
Well then, maybe Cloud Spanner
is the right call for you.

00:35:54.170 --> 00:35:57.020
But you'd really have
to test it out and see

00:35:57.020 --> 00:36:00.325
where does traditional
SQL start falling down,

00:36:00.325 --> 00:36:01.700
and when does
Cloud Spanner start

00:36:01.700 --> 00:36:04.033
to become worth it to you to
get the kind of performance

00:36:04.033 --> 00:36:06.720
that you can get out of it?

00:36:06.720 --> 00:36:09.860
So I'm going to
switch to security,

00:36:09.860 --> 00:36:12.240
last part of infrastructure.

00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:14.390
And one of the first
things people do,

00:36:14.390 --> 00:36:17.510
is how do I let people in
to the resources they want?

00:36:17.510 --> 00:36:20.150
And you use Cloud
IAM to do that.

00:36:20.150 --> 00:36:21.110
It's pretty basic.

00:36:21.110 --> 00:36:23.490
You've got a list of users.

00:36:23.490 --> 00:36:25.610
You can add permissions
and roles to them.

00:36:25.610 --> 00:36:27.050
And that's how you do it.

00:36:27.050 --> 00:36:29.540
The permissions and roles
definitely vary from product

00:36:29.540 --> 00:36:33.060
to product, and they are done
on a product to product basis.

00:36:33.060 --> 00:36:36.500
So different ones will
have different abilities

00:36:36.500 --> 00:36:39.580
to do things.

00:36:39.580 --> 00:36:42.210
For example, one of the
things that you can do

00:36:42.210 --> 00:36:45.400
is create custom roles.

00:36:45.400 --> 00:36:47.970
So you can take a whole
bunch of permissions

00:36:47.970 --> 00:36:51.483
and combine them into
a new custom role.

00:36:51.483 --> 00:36:53.400
But that's only available
for certain products

00:36:53.400 --> 00:36:56.460
and certain combinations,
so keep that in mind.

00:36:56.460 --> 00:36:59.530
We also have the ability to
do key management service.

00:36:59.530 --> 00:37:01.630
So if you want to
host your own keys,

00:37:01.630 --> 00:37:04.830
you can-- basically everything
on ours, in terms of data,

00:37:04.830 --> 00:37:08.040
is encrypted by default.
The only question you have

00:37:08.040 --> 00:37:10.560
is not whether or
not it's encrypted,

00:37:10.560 --> 00:37:13.222
it's whether or not you
manage the keys or we do.

00:37:13.222 --> 00:37:15.430
We'll do it by default.
We're happy to do it for you.

00:37:15.430 --> 00:37:18.960
We also know that
trust but verify.

00:37:18.960 --> 00:37:21.810
You're perfectly entitled
to host your own keys

00:37:21.810 --> 00:37:25.150
and manage your own keys if
that's what you want to do.

00:37:25.150 --> 00:37:27.850
All right, so now that's
all infrastructure,

00:37:27.850 --> 00:37:30.572
and that's all the stuff
that you absolutely have

00:37:30.572 --> 00:37:31.780
to have to move to the cloud.

00:37:31.780 --> 00:37:33.530
I'm going to talk about
some of the things

00:37:33.530 --> 00:37:37.180
that we can add to the picture,
because again, we're Google,

00:37:37.180 --> 00:37:40.990
and we have some experience
with some of these things.

00:37:40.990 --> 00:37:44.230
Not intelligence and not me,
but that's what we call it.

00:37:44.230 --> 00:37:47.170
So we'll start with
data analytics.

00:37:47.170 --> 00:37:50.680
So one of the first things
I'll show off is BigQuery.

00:37:50.680 --> 00:37:53.380
BigQuery is this amazing tool
that allows you basically

00:37:53.380 --> 00:38:03.430
to do very fast lookups of
large, huge, huge sets of data.

00:38:03.430 --> 00:38:06.100
And in fact, I'm
going to do one now.

00:38:06.100 --> 00:38:09.560
I'm going to brave the
demo gods once again.

00:38:09.560 --> 00:38:12.330
Load.

00:38:12.330 --> 00:38:15.510
All right, so I'm going
to open this query.

00:38:23.120 --> 00:38:24.700
I'm going to run this query.

00:38:24.700 --> 00:38:26.800
Now let's see.

00:38:26.800 --> 00:38:30.083
I'm going to show options,
yeah, that's fine, good.

00:38:30.083 --> 00:38:31.250
I'm going to run this query.

00:38:31.250 --> 00:38:32.530
So what am I running?

00:38:32.530 --> 00:38:36.490
I have all of Wikipedia's
LOBS loaded up.

00:38:36.490 --> 00:38:41.290
And so I can see the requests
for everything that's come in.

00:38:41.290 --> 00:38:44.745
And I can-- oh no,
it's not using cache.

00:38:44.745 --> 00:38:46.120
So this is going
to take a while.

00:38:46.120 --> 00:38:48.162
I'm just going to explain
what I was going to do.

00:38:48.162 --> 00:38:50.138
Basically, long
story short, I can

00:38:50.138 --> 00:38:51.680
in a relatively
short period of time,

00:38:51.680 --> 00:38:55.342
look through over a
trillion records in BigQuery

00:38:55.342 --> 00:38:56.800
and come up with
the answer of what

00:38:56.800 --> 00:39:01.780
are the top 100 most
searched things on Wikipedia

00:39:01.780 --> 00:39:05.020
for 2013 and 2014.

00:39:05.020 --> 00:39:07.450
I do know this is going to
take longer than most people's

00:39:07.450 --> 00:39:12.130
attention spans, because I had
hoped it would remain cached.

00:39:12.130 --> 00:39:13.330
But it didn't.

00:39:13.330 --> 00:39:16.900
So long story short,
this allows you

00:39:16.900 --> 00:39:20.980
to search through trillions of
records in just a few minutes,

00:39:20.980 --> 00:39:23.350
as opposed to maybe spinning
up your own data job

00:39:23.350 --> 00:39:26.500
and running it in
hours or whatnot.

00:39:26.500 --> 00:39:28.270
But I am going to,
better part of valor,

00:39:28.270 --> 00:39:31.200
and switch back to
my preso because I

00:39:31.200 --> 00:39:35.060
have one more demo to give,
and that one I'm happy about.

00:39:35.060 --> 00:39:38.240
So other data tools
that we have--

00:39:38.240 --> 00:39:38.810
Pub/Sub.

00:39:38.810 --> 00:39:40.070
Pub/Sub is our messaging bus.

00:39:40.070 --> 00:39:41.900
It's a publisher
subscriber model.

00:39:41.900 --> 00:39:43.622
You can do many to
many communication.

00:39:43.622 --> 00:39:45.080
Basically, we can
scale to hundreds

00:39:45.080 --> 00:39:47.660
of millions of operations,
hundreds of millions

00:39:47.660 --> 00:39:49.175
of messages per second.

00:39:49.175 --> 00:39:50.360
We also have Dataflow.

00:39:50.360 --> 00:39:52.880
Dataflow is a managed
service for data analysis.

00:39:52.880 --> 00:39:56.810
Basically, it's Apache
Beam, but managed by us.

00:39:56.810 --> 00:39:59.300
Also managed by us
is Dataproc, which

00:39:59.300 --> 00:40:04.500
is managed Spark and Hadoop
jobs, but with auto scaling.

00:40:04.500 --> 00:40:07.230
So it's important to note that
Cloud Pub/Sub and Cloud Storage

00:40:07.230 --> 00:40:09.760
form sort of a bus
so that you can--

00:40:09.760 --> 00:40:12.357
if you need to talk between
any of our services,

00:40:12.357 --> 00:40:14.190
that's probably going
to be one of these two

00:40:14.190 --> 00:40:16.770
that you use to do it,
with Cloud Storage being

00:40:16.770 --> 00:40:20.400
for unstructured data
like just a file,

00:40:20.400 --> 00:40:23.543
whereas Cloud Pub/Sub
will do messages for you.

00:40:23.543 --> 00:40:25.710
All right, we're going to
switch to machine learning

00:40:25.710 --> 00:40:26.877
and artificial intelligence.

00:40:26.877 --> 00:40:33.630
And typically we have three
kind of ways of using ML on GCP.

00:40:33.630 --> 00:40:35.560
The first is to use our models.

00:40:35.560 --> 00:40:37.390
The second is to create a model.

00:40:37.390 --> 00:40:40.860
And the third is to extend
and go further with models.

00:40:40.860 --> 00:40:44.550
So we talk about using models,
we have, some of our models

00:40:44.550 --> 00:40:47.100
that we use the
most already exposed

00:40:47.100 --> 00:40:50.370
in the form of these ML APIs--

00:40:50.370 --> 00:40:52.590
Vision API, Natural
Language API, Speech API,

00:40:52.590 --> 00:40:55.300
Translation API,
there are a few more.

00:40:55.300 --> 00:40:57.270
But basically what
they allow you to do,

00:40:57.270 --> 00:40:59.125
is you want to detect faces.

00:40:59.125 --> 00:41:01.500
We have a Vision APIs that
will allow you to detect faces

00:41:01.500 --> 00:41:03.030
in your apps--

00:41:03.030 --> 00:41:05.100
I'm sorry, in your
pictures, because that's

00:41:05.100 --> 00:41:06.190
a very common use case.

00:41:06.190 --> 00:41:09.510
Same thing with logo detection
or language detection,

00:41:09.510 --> 00:41:11.940
translation, all of that stuff.

00:41:11.940 --> 00:41:14.610
Now typically what happens is,
someone uses this and like,

00:41:14.610 --> 00:41:16.450
this is great and
it does this thing,

00:41:16.450 --> 00:41:18.550
but I really wish it could
do a little bit more.

00:41:18.550 --> 00:41:20.820
Case in point,
someone came to me

00:41:20.820 --> 00:41:25.170
and said they wanted Vision
API, but they wanted it trained

00:41:25.170 --> 00:41:27.270
so it could detect
whether or not

00:41:27.270 --> 00:41:30.990
someone's face was covered,
because they wrote security cam

00:41:30.990 --> 00:41:34.020
software and they wanted to
be able to detect someone

00:41:34.020 --> 00:41:37.690
masked coming into a location,
because that's usually bad,

00:41:37.690 --> 00:41:38.190
right?

00:41:38.190 --> 00:41:39.090
We all agree.

00:41:39.090 --> 00:41:41.040
Security, someone
coming in a mask,

00:41:41.040 --> 00:41:42.800
they're not there for fun--

00:41:42.800 --> 00:41:46.350
or your fun, I guess.

00:41:46.350 --> 00:41:48.600
So we also have this thing
called Auto ML, which

00:41:48.600 --> 00:41:52.410
takes some of our
APIs and allows

00:41:52.410 --> 00:41:54.210
you to train your
own models, but based

00:41:54.210 --> 00:41:55.800
within narrow constraints.

00:41:55.800 --> 00:41:58.800
And so I'm actually
going to show one.

00:41:58.800 --> 00:42:03.067
And it is a demo near and
dear to my heart called

00:42:03.067 --> 00:42:03.900
Pizza Authenticator.

00:42:03.900 --> 00:42:06.300
So I'm going to
switch over to laptop.

00:42:06.300 --> 00:42:08.460
We're going to refresh.

00:42:08.460 --> 00:42:10.460
And I'm going to explain.

00:42:10.460 --> 00:42:12.360
So I was trying to
teach myself ML,

00:42:12.360 --> 00:42:13.960
and I'm not from California.

00:42:13.960 --> 00:42:15.400
I'm from Philadelphia.

00:42:15.400 --> 00:42:17.470
And whenever I'm here--

00:42:17.470 --> 00:42:18.460
I live here now--

00:42:18.460 --> 00:42:21.187
I really don't enjoy pizza here.

00:42:21.187 --> 00:42:23.520
And if you're from California,
you don't understand why.

00:42:23.520 --> 00:42:24.580
And if you're not
from California,

00:42:24.580 --> 00:42:25.622
you're nodding your head.

00:42:25.622 --> 00:42:27.160
You understand.

00:42:27.160 --> 00:42:28.127
So I wanted to write--

00:42:28.127 --> 00:42:30.460
could I write something that
would detect whether or not

00:42:30.460 --> 00:42:32.230
pizza was authentic?

00:42:32.230 --> 00:42:33.935
Like if it said it
was New York style,

00:42:33.935 --> 00:42:35.060
it is New York style pizza.

00:42:35.060 --> 00:42:37.250
If it's Chicago style,
it's Chicago style.

00:42:37.250 --> 00:42:39.368
So we're going to see
here on my desktop.

00:42:39.368 --> 00:42:40.660
I'm going to pull up a picture.

00:42:45.220 --> 00:42:47.710
And here we go.

00:42:47.710 --> 00:42:50.400
So this pizza comes
from my local pizza

00:42:50.400 --> 00:42:52.080
place that is very authentic.

00:42:52.080 --> 00:42:57.130
Like I think it's very
good for this area pizza.

00:42:57.130 --> 00:43:00.470
So I'm going to go ahead
and upload a picture of it.

00:43:00.470 --> 00:43:01.830
There we go.

00:43:01.830 --> 00:43:05.270
And we have an auto ML model
that's going to go look at it.

00:43:05.270 --> 00:43:07.755
First it's determining whether
or not it's pizza or not.

00:43:07.755 --> 00:43:10.130
And then after it figures out
that it is, in fact, pizza,

00:43:10.130 --> 00:43:11.213
it should give me a score.

00:43:11.213 --> 00:43:13.860
And it says wow, that is
95% New York style pizza.

00:43:13.860 --> 00:43:16.430
That is good pizza, at
least according to me.

00:43:16.430 --> 00:43:20.840
I was able to train this model
without any ML knowledge.

00:43:20.840 --> 00:43:23.300
I was able to just take
pictures, classify them,

00:43:23.300 --> 00:43:24.110
put them in.

00:43:24.110 --> 00:43:26.000
And it wasn't that
tremendously hard.

00:43:26.000 --> 00:43:28.730
And now I have my
own Auto ML model

00:43:28.730 --> 00:43:31.382
that can determine whether or
not I'm going to like pizza.

00:43:31.382 --> 00:43:32.840
If you want to play
with this, this

00:43:32.840 --> 00:43:35.270
is also in the Developer Zone.

00:43:35.270 --> 00:43:38.870
You can go, and we have at
various times that we're not

00:43:38.870 --> 00:43:41.310
announcing, we're
bringing in actual pizza.

00:43:41.310 --> 00:43:43.160
And when there's
not actual pizza,

00:43:43.160 --> 00:43:45.170
you can use it with
pictures of pizza

00:43:45.170 --> 00:43:47.330
which, we know where
they came from.

00:43:47.330 --> 00:43:50.843
But you can play with it and
kind of see how this works.

00:43:50.843 --> 00:43:52.260
It's also a GitHub
project, so you

00:43:52.260 --> 00:43:56.150
can go and download it and
build your own model and play.

00:43:56.150 --> 00:44:01.700
If want to rate other
tools, other regional foods,

00:44:01.700 --> 00:44:03.190
you can do that.

00:44:03.190 --> 00:44:03.980
It's cool.

00:44:03.980 --> 00:44:08.250
I'm going to switch back to
the preso and close it out.

00:44:08.250 --> 00:44:11.090
So if you want to
go further and not

00:44:11.090 --> 00:44:13.250
train a narrowly
constrained model,

00:44:13.250 --> 00:44:14.870
you probably want
to use TensorFlow,

00:44:14.870 --> 00:44:16.640
and Cloud ML Engine
is probably the way

00:44:16.640 --> 00:44:18.020
to do that on our platform.

00:44:18.020 --> 00:44:19.370
It's scalable.

00:44:19.370 --> 00:44:22.460
It's managed TensorFlow where
we will make recommendations

00:44:22.460 --> 00:44:27.575
on how to run those jobs.

00:44:27.575 --> 00:44:29.200
All right, so we're
at the conclusions,

00:44:29.200 --> 00:44:31.220
and I'm close on
time, but I'm there.

00:44:31.220 --> 00:44:34.750
So there are a ton
of GCP products.

00:44:34.750 --> 00:44:36.130
So we weren't here.

00:44:36.130 --> 00:44:39.250
We only showed these products.

00:44:39.250 --> 00:44:40.660
We didn't show everything.

00:44:40.660 --> 00:44:43.750
And here, we only showed these.

00:44:43.750 --> 00:44:47.500
And here, we only showed these.

00:44:47.500 --> 00:44:48.980
I didn't get into all of it.

00:44:48.980 --> 00:44:50.980
But my hope is that
now you have a better

00:44:50.980 --> 00:44:53.717
idea of where the tools are
and what kind of problems

00:44:53.717 --> 00:44:55.300
you can solve with
them, and certainly

00:44:55.300 --> 00:44:59.470
to compute at another level,
you can get to the resources

00:44:59.470 --> 00:45:00.760
that you need.

00:45:00.760 --> 00:45:03.490
So with that, I'll say thank
you very much for coming.

